<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Trucial States</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1820">1820</a>–<a href="/years/1971">1971</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Trucial States were the sheikhdoms of the southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-gulf">Persian Gulf</a> coast that signed maritime truces with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> from 1820 onwards, culminating in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perpetual-maritime-truce">Perpetual Maritime Truce</a> of 1853 and the Exclusive Agreement of 1892, which made them British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorates</a> in all but name. Britain handled their foreign relations and defence until it withdrew in 1971, when six of them formed the United Arab <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirates</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ras-al-khaimah">Ras al-Khaimah</a> joined in 1972.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/trucial-states.jpg" alt="Trucial States" width="320" height="174" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Svyatoslav · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Trucial_States_(1968%E2%80%931971).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The British called the south-eastern coast of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-gulf">Persian Gulf</a> the Pirate Coast, arguing that raiders based there, particularly the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-qasimi">Qawasim</a> or Al Qasimi, the ruling families of Sharjah and Ras Al Khaimah, harassed British-flagged shipping. The first of many skirmishes came in 1797, when the snow Bassein was seized and released two days later. British expeditions to protect Indian trade struck Ras Al Khaimah and other harbours in 1809 and again, with far greater destructive force, in 1819.</p><p>A peace treaty was signed in January 1820 and adhered to by all the sheikhs of the coast. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan-bin-saqr-al-qasimi">Sultan bin Saqr Al Qasimi</a> of Sharjah signed on 6 January, and Shakhbut bin Diyab Al Nahyan signed for his son Tahnoon, ruler of Abu Dhabi, on 11 January. The rulers of Ajman and Umm Al Quwain acceded on 15 March aboard the ship of Major-General William Keir Grant.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The treaty was not a conspicuous success: conflicts that the British considered raids continued until 1835, when the sheikhs agreed not to fight at sea. After a ten-year truce in 1843 they signed the Perpetual Treaty of Maritime Peace in 1853, overseen by Captain A. B. Kemball. Treaties of 1847 and 1856 undertook to abolish the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a>. In 1892, mainly in reaction to the ambitions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, six rulers signed the Exclusive Agreement, promising not to dispose of territory except to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> nor to deal with other foreign governments without its consent. Britain promised protection against attack by sea and help in case of attack by land.</p><p>Aeroplanes arrived in the 1920s as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> sought an air route to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, and an airfield opened at Sharjah in 1932. Kalba was granted trucial status in 1936 as the site of a back-up landing strip and rejoined Sharjah in 1951. Fujairah was recognised in 1952 after pressure from Petroleum Concessions Limited, which wanted a free hand to explore for oil along the east coast. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trucial-states-council">Trucial States Council</a>, a consultative forum presided over by the British Political Agent, first met in 1952 and received a British grant in 1965.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>On 16 January 1968 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harold-wilson">Harold Wilson</a> announced that British troops would be withdrawn from east of Suez. Sheikh Zayed bin Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-nahyan">Al Nahyan</a> of Abu Dhabi and Sheikh Rashid of Dubai agreed the principle of a union on 18 February 1968, and after two years of often stormy talks, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahrain">Bahrain</a> and Qatar dropped out, leaving six <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirates</a> to agree on union on 18 July 1971. The Act of Union was signed on 2 December 1971, and Ras Al Khaimah joined on 10 February 1972, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> seized the Tunb islands and occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-musa">Abu Musa</a> on 30 November 1971.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Trucial States” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The treaties its rulers signed with Britain from 1820 onwards defined the system of the <b>Trucial States</b>, under which local dynasties kept internal authority while Britain controlled their foreign relations and maritime affairs, a system that shaped the political map of the lower Gulf until 1971.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sheikhdom-of-sharjah">Sheikhdom of Sharjah · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The United Arab Emirates grew out of the <b>Trucial States</b>, a group of sheikhdoms on the southern shore of the Gulf bound to Britain by the General Maritime Treaty of 1820, the Perpetual Maritime Truce of 1853 and the Exclusive Agreement of 1892, under which Britain conducted their…</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-arab-emirates">United Arab Emirates · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Six of the <b>Trucial States</b>, led by Abu Dhabi and Dubai, formed the United Arab Emirates after Britain's withdrawal from the Gulf.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-2">2 December in history</a></li></ul></section>
